-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1 (corrected: sum to $k$) — KMT strong embedding of the random walk
-- statement:
--   Let $\rho$ be a law on $\mathbb R$ with mean $0$, variance $1$, and a moment generating function finite in a neighbourhood of $0$. Then there are constants $C,K,\lambda>0$, depending only on $\rho$, such that: given any standard Brownian motion $B$ on any probability space, one can construct from $B$ an iid sequence $X_1,X_2,\dots$ with law $\rho$ (each $X_i$ a measurable function of the path of $B$) such that for every $n\ge1$ and $x>0$,
--
--   $$P\Bigl(\max_{1\le k\le n}\Bigl|\sum_{i=1}^{k}X_i-B_k\Bigr|>C\log n+x\Bigr)<Ke^{-\lambda x}.$$
--
--   This is the Komlós–Major–Tusnády theorem; all the couplings of the mission come from it.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The mgf condition is integrability of $e^{ly}$ for $|l|<\delta$. The constants are chosen before the probability space and the Brownian motion.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: |Σ_{i=1}^n X_i − B_n| under sup_{1≤k≤n} (15) → |Σ_{i=1}^k X_i − B_k| under max_{1≤k≤n}; reason: the printed term does not depend on k; (14), p.10, and (63), p.24, show the index k
--   - encoding: "given a Brownian motion B, and using it, one can construct" → for every probability space (Ω : Type u) and Brownian motion B there are X_i, each measurable for the σ-algebra generated by the path of B; reason: this is what constructing the X_i from B means
--   - encoding: P(sup … > a) → P of the event that some k ∈ [1, n] exceeds a; reason: the same event; no real supremum is needed
-- source:
--   Chakraborty and Honnappa, Strong Embeddings for Transitory Queueing Models, arXiv:1906.06740v1, Theorem 3.1, eq. (15), p.10

import Mathlib
open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal
universe u

namespace TransitoryQueue

/-- **Theorem 3.1 (KMT strong embedding of the random walk).** Let `ρ` be a law on `ℝ` with mean
`0`, variance `1` and a moment generating function finite in a neighbourhood of `0`. There are
constants `C, K, λ > 0` depending only on `ρ` such that, given any standard Brownian motion `B`,
one can construct from `B` an iid sequence `X_1, X_2, …` with law `ρ` such that for every
`n ≥ 1` and `x > 0`, `P(max_{1≤k≤n} |Σ_{i=1}^{k} X_i - B_k| > C log n + x) < K e^{-λ x}`.
Chakraborty and Honnappa, *Strong Embeddings for Transitory Queueing Models*, arXiv:1906.06740v1, Theorem 3.1, p.10 (Komlós–Major–Tusnády).

**Formalization Note** The printed (15) has `Σ_{i=1}^{n} X_i - B_n` under `sup_{1≤k≤n}`; the
index is `k` (see (14), p.10, and (63), p.24). "Using it" is that every `X_i` is measurable with
respect to the σ-algebra generated by the path of `B`. The constants come before the probability
space. "max > a" is written "some `k` has value > a". -/
theorem theorem_3_1 (ρ : Measure ℝ) [IsProbabilityMeasure ρ]
    (hmgf : ∃ δ : ℝ, 0 < δ ∧ ∀ l : ℝ, |l| < δ → Integrable (fun y => Real.exp (l * y)) ρ)
    (hmean : ∫ y, y ∂ρ = 0) (hvar : ∫ y, y ^ 2 ∂ρ = 1) :
    ∃ C K lam : ℝ, 0 < C ∧ 0 < K ∧ 0 < lam ∧
      ∀ (Ω : Type u) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
        (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ), IsBrownianReal B P →
        ∃ X : ℕ → Ω → ℝ, (∀ i, HasLaw (X i) ρ P) ∧ iIndepFun X P ∧
          (∀ i, Measurable[MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω (s : ℝ≥0) => B s ω) inferInstance]
            (X i)) ∧
          ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → ∀ x : ℝ, 0 < x →
            P.real {ω | ∃ k ∈ Finset.Icc 1 n, C * Real.log n + x <
                |∑ i ∈ Finset.range k, X i ω - B (k : ℝ≥0) ω|}
              < K * Real.exp (-(lam * x)) := by sorry

end TransitoryQueue
